Post-Viral Reactive Arthropathy and Musculoskeletal Syndromes
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Severe symmetrical peripheral polyarthralgia, Intense early morning joint stiffness, History of preceding viral infection, Normal joint radiographs/negative autoimmune serology

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-039
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe, incapacitating joint stiffness and pain preventing ambulation, requiring urgent assessment for an acute inflammatory flare or septic arthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient who awoke completely unable to walk or use their hands due to agonizing, symmetrical joint stiffness and pain following a recent viral illness. Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out acute destructive joint pathology, exclude severe systemic autoimmune disease, and provide immediate pharmacological mobility restoration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I had a virus a month ago, and now my hands and knees are locked up completely; I can't even get out of bed."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a distinct history of a severe, acute viral infection (fever, rash, fatigue) exactly 4 weeks ago. Over the past 3 days, there has been a migrating, distinctly additive pattern of severe joint involvement. Today, the patient woke with severe, highly distinct, and highly symmetrical polyarthralgia affecting the wrists, knees, and ankles. The patient describes intense, profound generalized joint stiffness specifically peaking early in the morning, which noticeably improves only after hours of painful movement. The joint pain is accompanied by severe, unrelenting extreme generalized fatigue. Complete absence of previous joint issues.
Functional Status: Bedbound initially, slowly ambulating with a severe shuffling gait by the time of the visit.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; unable to perform basic self-care tasks or prepare food due to severe hand involvement.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, visibly stiff, holding hands in a guarded position, moving with extreme, deliberate caution.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, symmetrical tenderness and restricted range of motion in the bilateral wrists, MCPs, and knees. Transient, distinct, but mild soft tissue swelling localizes precisely to the tenosynovium. Complete absence of massive, severely destructive joint effusions, severe erythema, or frank severe purulent arthritis. No focal warmth. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Severe symmetrical peripheral polyarthralgia, Intense early morning joint stiffness, History of preceding viral infection, and Normal joint radiographs/negative autoimmune serology (pending outpatient labs).
Environmental Observations: Unopened food packaging on the counter due to poor grip strength.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took one dose of paracetamol with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Reactive Arthropathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, sudden onset directly following a distinct viral illness, though clinically it is indistinguishable from early RA without formal autoimmune serology (RF, anti-CCP).

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symmetrical, polyarticular involvement lacking a single, massively swollen, bright red, hot joint, and the absence of high fever.

Osteoarthritis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound early morning stiffness (lasting hours, not minutes), the inflammatory soft-tissue swelling, and the sudden onset in non-weight-bearing joints (wrists).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe functional limitation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severely debilitating symmetrical polyarthropathy directly linked to a recent viral illness. The sudden, intense functional impairment caused by the profound morning stiffness necessitated an urgent clinical assessment. A physical examination was mandatory to rule out single-joint catastrophic infections (septic arthritis), differentiate from a severe new-onset autoimmune crisis, and immediately prescribe scheduled, high-dose anti-inflammatory therapy to restore basic functional mobility and independence. Leaving the patient immobilized and in severe pain until the next in-hours period was clinically unacceptable, fulfilling the urgent after-hours criteria (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe strict, scheduled high-dose NSAIDs (e.g., Celecoxib 200mg daily or Naproxen 500mg twice daily with food) to immediately suppress the tenosynovial inflammation. Add Paracetamol for breakthrough analgesia. Urgent referral for baseline joint radiographs and comprehensive autoimmune serology (RF, anti-CCP, ANA, CRP) to definitively exclude primary Rheumatoid Arthritis.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the NSAID medication exactly at 8:00 PM with a snack and immediately upon waking, the patient will blunt the overnight inflammatory cascade; waking up with significantly reduced morning stiffness and restored hand function will powerfully reinforce this strict twice-daily dosing schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Adapted utensils if grip strength remains poor.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days to review blood/x-ray results and monitor symptom trajectory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if a single specific joint suddenly becomes intensely hot, bright red, and massively swollen, or if an unremitting high fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically presumed reactive; formal autoimmune testing is absolutely required to rule out Rheumatoid Arthritis.
Clinical Course: The immune system's localized inflammatory response in the joints typically waxes and wanes for several months before resolving completely without causing permanent joint destruction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your joints are fiercely inflamed but not permanently damaged. If one specific joint suddenly swells out of proportion to the others and feels burning hot to the touch, you must go to the emergency department immediately to rule out a severe bacterial infection in the joint fluid.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: After fighting off your recent virus, your immune system became confused and is now temporarily attacking the lining of your own joints. The treatment is not a cure for the virus, but rather massive doses of anti-inflammatory medication to suppress your immune system's localized attack and give you back your mobility.
Actionable Steps: Take the NSAIDs precisely on schedule with food. Gently keep moving the joints through their range of motion throughout the day; absolute rest will actually worsen the stiffness.

